  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B043",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B021",
    "B024"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B043 remains the most complete and sharply delimited achievement: the magic-wavelength lattice concept enabled interrogation of many atoms without the usual trapping perturbation, and its realization has produced independently reproducible clocks of extraordinary accuracy and stability. It represents fundamental control of atomic quantum systems, not simply improved timekeeping, and its capacity for precision tests is mature without requiring us to claim that those tests have already answered every physical question. Katori and Ye remain an unusually coherent two-person attribution to the conceptual invention and decisive experimental realization.",
    "case_against": "The prize must be restricted to the original lattice-clock invention and realization. It should not absorb frequency-comb work, other clock architectures, comparison techniques, or the broad subsequent metrological ecosystem. The historical record should also confirm that the respective Katori and Ye roles adequately represent the indispensable conceptual and experimental steps.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that the Aharonov-Bohm and Berry effects revealed the observable physical significance of quantum phase and have broad experimental confirmation.",
        "response": "I continue to regard B021 as a profound alternative, especially because phase information is central across atomic, optical, and condensed-matter physics. But the shared lesson does not erase the separate historical discoveries. Until a citation can join them without implying a single event or blurring prior recognition of related topological ideas, B043 offers the cleaner public achievement and credit line."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes the independently reproduced performance of optical lattice clocks and the clear division between Katori's magic-wavelength concept and Ye's defining experimental development.",
        "response": "I agree with this assessment. The chair's boundary question is important precisely because the achievement is so productive: its later metrological consequences must not be used as a substitute for identifying the original breakthrough. Framed as invention and realization of optical lattice clocks, however, the concept, experimental demonstration, reproducibility, and attribution remain exceptionally well aligned."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson prefers aberration-corrected electron optics as a bounded invention with transformative experimental reach, while noting unresolved credit among Rose, Haider, and Urban.",
        "response": "B046 is a serious instrumental alternative and illustrates why enabling technologies can merit recognition. Its unresolved three-person historical allocation is nevertheless material. B043 presently has the more secure two-person account while matching that standard of mature, independently established experimental transformation."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether a historical audit can draw the boundary between the original magic-wavelength lattice-clock breakthrough and later metrological developments with complete precision.",
      "Whether the documentation of Katori's and Ye's respective indispensable contributions is sufficient to exclude additional foundational credit."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict requiring recusal is apparent from the supplied profile. My collaborations in attosecond physics do not involve the proposed B043 laureates."
  }
}
